format session-log-v1
stamp 20260823.090034
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title One word for a place you stop
prompt Keaton asked to rename cairn to checkpoint. I found checkpoint already seated and asked; he answered approve only all checkpoint, so the rename lands and the word carries both senses.
think Measured before moving and found the collision rather than the empty space I expected. checkpoint already stood in the Lexicon as a named stop-before-cross gate with its own closing form check in (checkpoint) and 858 uses -- so this rename MERGES two senses rather than moving a name, the opposite of what crux to construction did an hour earlier.
think Raised it once, because a rename that creates ambiguity is the thing the previous rename existed to remove. Keaton chose the merge, so the job was to make it a decision rather than a drift: the Lexicon now carries one entry reading a marked place where you stop, with both readings numbered under it and the reason they share that sense -- one stops you before you cross forward, the other so you can walk back.
think Recorded the free alternative inside the entry and the checkpoint row. trailhead measured zero uses tree-wide on this date, so if the senses ever want separating the word is already found and the measurement is already taken.
obs The rename left two guards red and both were the same trap in different clothes. A dated session log is named 20260809-025109_debride-old-marks-seat-cairn.bron, and the case-insensitive sweep rewrote living references to it as seat-checkpoint.bron, pointing at a file that cannot exist. This is the glow-os lesson arriving one round later: a rename must always ask which occurrences MEAN the thing being renamed.
obs Three renames, three different answers to that question. crux: the seated word survives, the path moves. glow-os: the dated filename survives, the directory moves. cairn: the dated filename survives again, and this time the word itself is what moves. The shape repeats; the boundary never sits in the same place twice.
obs A merged Lexicon entry beats two rows with one term. Two rows would have read as an oversight to the next reader and been silently re-split; one row that names the merge and dates it is a decision that stays decided.
loom rename occurrences=821 files=255 living=28 testimony_untouched=227 lexicon_rows_merged=2 guards_red=2 guards_fixed=2 census_before=194 census_after=192 ceiling=192
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md the ledger, renamed from CAIRNS.md
file .claude/rules/checkpoint.md the rule and its Cursor twin, renamed
file context/LEXICON.md two rows merged into one entry carrying both senses, with trailhead named as the free word
recommend keep-going -- rename three of four is Skate to Surf with Surf Social, then the name change as a deep debride last so one history rewrite covers everything. The very large remainder of the vision -- Druid Style for the frontend, the fundraising documents, the spellbook, Lotus and the magazine -- still wants one expanded prompt that ranks it, which is the round I would take after the renames land.
